Theory Concept in Brief 
Introduction to Backup 
A backup is a copy of database data stored separately so that it can be restored when 
needed. Backups protect against accidental deletion, hardware crashes, and system 
failures. 
Types of Backup in MySQL 
1. Logical Backup  
o Created using SQL statements.  
o Example: mysqldump utility.  
o Produces .sql files.  
2. Physical Backup  
o Copies actual database files.  
o Faster for large databases.  
3. Full Backup  
o Entire database is backed up.  
4. Incremental Backup  
o Only changes since the last backup are stored. 
Backup Command Example 
mysqldump -u root -p database_name > backup_file.sql 
Example: 
mysqldump -u root -p college_db &gt; college_backup.sql 
Recovery Command Example 
mysql -u root -p database_name < backup_file.sql 
Example: 
mysql -u root -p college_db &lt; college_backup.sql 
Importance of Recovery 
Recovery ensures that data can be restored quickly after failure, reducing downtime and 
preventing permanent data loss. 
Conclusion 
Backup and recovery are essential parts of database management. Regular backups 
protect valuable data from unexpected failures and mistakes. MySQL provides simple 
and effective tools like mysqldump for backup and restoration. By practicing these 
operations, users can ensure data availability, security, and reliable disaster recovery. 
